notes={
'ginkgo':{'concept':'Seven fingerlike conceptual tips and unequal branch-like vein hierarchy were not copied. Final follows the pre-frozen correction to six elongate lobes and finer dichotomous venation.','visual':['hero: Six separated elongate rounded lobes, deeply incised fan base and visible fine branching veins. Petiole is narrow and connected.','reversehero: Continuous rear fan has no accidental leaf holes; subtle volume rim is visible.','front: All six lobes, five sinuses, deepest central division and fine front-side forks read clearly.','side: Thin full-volume blade and petiole have a continuous profile; not a missing/backface surface.','rear: Six-lobe identity is maintained with the naturally undecorated reverse face.','top: Slight fold and thin thickness visible; expected strongly foreshortened plate, not clipping.','clay: Original relief veins remain visible without color-based separation; six lobe silhouette persists.','silhouette: Distinct continuous basal fan and narrow petiole; no modern broad biloba or branch.'],'factual':'HKU CH105 source photograph and local official page inspected. The page identifies Ginkgo yimaensis, Middle Jurassic China. Photo supports a deeply divided isolated organ and long petiole, but individual fine veins/lobe boundaries are not uniformly resolvable. Six lobes and four terminal veins per lobe are expressly illustrative choices, not counts measured on CH105. Original taxonomic paper remains unavailable; no exact specimen cast or modern biloba/short-shoot claim.','scope_limit':'Specimen-inspired isolated organ only; no complete plant, reproductive structure, or species-wide universal lobe count.','specific':['Actual outline has six radial peaks and five sinuses; central sinus radius0.017018m versus others about0.0340m.','All42 actual connected vein segments, each9five-vertex rings, physically cross and protrude from lamina. All42 segments also have full-object exposed surface witnesses. Front and clay pixels corroborate useful relief, not merely named groups.']},
'dicranophyllum':{'concept':'Dense scaly column and variable conceptual fork counts were deliberately not copied. The final preserves a slender open helical axis with selected mature twice-forking leaves.','visual':['hero: Slender slightly curved unbranched axis remains visible through open foliage; very fine distal leaf detail is delicate at512.','reversehero: Distributed foliage and axis continuity confirmed; no mass of bark-scale pedestals.','front: Whole plant is framed with margin; many paired forks readable, but all192tips cannot honestly be counted at this scale.','side: Sparse radial distribution, pointed terminal leaf form and attached leaves visible.','rear: Reverse foliage has no missing surface sheets; axis remains coherent.','top: Radial helical distribution supported; dense top overlap is expected and is not an endpoint-count proof.','clay: Thin forked structures persist without palette cues; this does not establish exact tip count by itself.','silhouette: Branching narrow leaves, rather than a broad leaf silhouette or massive tree crown.'],'factual':'Primary Van Der Pas et al2017 text and original Fig6/12 personally inspected. Narrow leaves with successive forks and usually wider second fork are supported; reconstruction of a single unbranched habit remains interpretive, not a whole fossil observation. Geometry measured here has first-fork angles about17.156degrees and second-fork angles about24.054degrees, within the primary described ranges. Nominal1.6m axis and48leaves are art choices; no roots, reproduction or asserted family identity.','scope_limit':'512whole-plant views communicate habit, not every fine tip or microscopic cushion. Exact fork verification uses all48 unchanged source-subset detail images plus complete-source graph and contact tests. Subset images are not claimed to be full-scene evidence.','specific':['Each of48 actual seven-island leaf graphs has four distinct terminal nodes and three degree3forks;192tips/144forks, minimum pair separation0.007150434m. All48 tip morphologies personally inspected in four native-resolution12tile sheets.','Every leaf and cushion has actual stem penetration. Initial triangle-centroid-only test lacked cushion witnesses for18groups; denser barycentric sampling found genuine exposed surfaces for all48.','Cushion09 is the least exposed (only1of440 dense triangle samples). Full-source original-material witness-directed25mm and8mmfield renders show a small attached diamond;00/10/17 confirm broader/marginal cases. These are real pixels, not a claim that cushions are distinguishable in the512whole-plant view.','Targeted source-subset leaf diagnostic frames deliberately emphasize forks and may crop the basal segment; full-source contact frames and original whole-object views supply attachment/overall-form coverage.']},
'noeggerathia':{'concept':'Too many conceptual pairs and central pinnule midribs were rejected before freeze. Final uses12pairs, distal shallow dentition and open fan forks without a pinnule midrib.','visual':['hero: Rounded wedge pinnules enlarge through middle and narrow at both basal and terminal sets; rachis remains visible.','reversehero: Open radiating venation visible on the decorated surface, not a central pinnule midrib.','front:24attached pinnules with small basal and terminal pairs; opposite mostly plain surfaces are expected.','side: Thin closed organ with continuous rachis; no unsupported long bare distal spike.','rear: Large pinnules show distinct fan veins and broad distal margins.','top: Thin relief and attached rachis profile corroborated under foreshortening.','clay: Wedge-rounded shape and size sequence remain clear without palette cues; fine relief subtle.','silhouette: Entire frond tapering profile and shallow distal scallops preserved; no needle-like terminal pinnules.'],'factual':'The2003 author-uploaded primary text was independently read online; it supports fan venation, distal dentation/lobing, small basal pairs and narrowing terminal pinnules. Official2021 National Museum Plate22 Fig1(NM-E122a) was personally viewed; it supports broad attached pinnules but does not resolve fine teeth. Figures2–5 were excluded. Fine2003 plate pixels remain uninspected. This is one sterile frond interpretation; complete length and12pairs are artistic, not a holotype cast.','scope_limit':'Fine marginal teeth are text-supported conservative interpretation, not verified pixels from the inaccessible2003 plate or reliably resolved2021 photo. No trunk, roots, terminal cone or cuticle claims.','specific':['Actual24 single closed pinnule islands and24 fan-vein systems, with one rachis. Full transformed outlines recorded, not config-derived counts.','All24pinnules contact actual rachis; all24vein systems contact their corresponding blades and have full-object surface witnesses. Rear/reversehero show useful fan relief.','The final small pair reaches the tip region; prior long bare-axis failure remains preserved.']},
'bjuvia':{'concept':'Entire strap silhouette retained, but thick rope-like conceptual midrib and blade were reduced. No conceptual whole plant adopted.','visual':['hero: Broad entire elongated blade, continuous proportionate midrib and dense near-transverse open veins.','reversehero: Undivided reverse blade continuous; no false pinnation.','front: Entire margin, narrowed base/rounded tapering apex, and clearly nonreticulate transverse stripes.','side: Thin closed lamina and relief rib remain connected.','rear: Reverse leaf surface continuous; original bounds respected.','top: Thin plate and central rib relief visible; no slab substitution.','clay: Fine open vein relief persists without palette; no mesh-like crossveins.','silhouette: Entire unsegmented strap outline; no palm-style pinnate foliage.'],'factual':'Harris1961 scannedpp314–315 were personally read, retaining the circumstantial rather than organically attached whole-plant association. The Komlo2021 Fig11 fossil photograph was personally viewed and is Jurassic comparative morphology, not claimed identical to Triassic material; adjacent whole-plant painting was excluded. Glossopteris primary PlateIV pixels/text were used solely as comparison. Entire strap blade, central rib and dense open transverse veins are supported; complete outline/0.5m/96rows remain illustrative.','scope_limit':'Isolated leaf organ only; no trunk, rosette, reproductive association or exact complete specimen reconstruction. Outline and color alone are insufficient distinction from Glossopteris.','specific':['Actual one lamina, one midrib and192 independent three-ring lateral tubes in96rows; independent full triangle BVHs show no cross-row lateral surface intersections. All contacts and exposed surfaces confirmed.','Maker comparison excluded the Glossopteris slab but used Bjuvia whole bounds including the midrib cap, overshooting lamina length by5.621101536micro-meters. Original evidence retained.','Independent replacement evidence measures lamina-material vertices only for both exact GLBs, keeps all Bjuvia geometry and only explicitly declared Glossopteris leaf/vein subset. Six new neutral same-camera/light views personally inspected; open near-transverse lines versus crosslinked polygonal net are unequivocal in closeups.','Post-transform readback is0.4999999850988388m(Bjuvia) versus0.5m(Glossopteris), not bitwise identical. Difference1.49011612e-8m is one binary32 endpoint ULP at0.25m, about0.0000132pixel in512/0.58m full view. This quantization is explicitly disclosed, not a relaxed candidate dimension bound.']}}
